Consider this situation...

	##@#f##o##
	  ^ ^  ^
	You |  a goblin bent on your destruction
	your pet

When the goblin throws a weapon towards you, it first checks to see if
it hits your pet.  If not, the weapon continues on its trajectory and
has a chance of hitting you.  When you throw something in his
direction, it only checks to see if the pet is hit.  If not, the
weapon you threw falls to the ground.

This does not address the problem of a monster being able to step on a
weapon, pick it up, and throw it all in one turn.

